:"to shoot","H4513":"to withhold","H1250":"grain","H5344":"to pierce","H3816":"people","H7218":"head","H7666":"to buy grain","H7836":"to seek","H1245":"to seek","H1875":"to seek","H5929":"leaf","H6524":"to sprout","H1004":"place","H5157":"to inherit","H5650":"servant/slave","H191":"fool(ish)","H2450":"wise","H6529":"fruit","H6086":"wood","H3947":"take","H2005":"look!","H776":"country/planet","H7999":"to complete","H637":"also","H2398":"to sin"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"bede","Balance. Both in commerce, (Deuteronomy xxv. 13.; Calmet) and in passing sentence on others. (Ven. Bede)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Wisdom. God resists the proud, chap. xvi. 18., and xviii. 12., and James iv. 6."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Solicitous. Or ambitious. Hebrew, “the potent,” or Septuagint, “the impious.”"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","For him. As comparatively nothing worth to his master, chap. xxi. 18., and Isaias xliii. 3"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Overthrown. Ten just men would have saved Sodom. Achan alone threw all Israel into confusion. (Calmet)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Mean. Literally, “indigent” of sense. (Haydock) — We must put up with some faults, as none are without. (Horace i. Sat. 3.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Walketh. Septuagint, “the double-tongued,” dissembler, or great talker. (Calmet)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","That is. Hebrew, “that hateth those who make agreements is secure.” (Mont.[Montanus?])"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Gracious. Virtuous and beautiful, as God hath granted beauty also for good purposes. This and virtue tend to the advancement of women, while men can use their strength to acquire riches. (Calmet) — Glory. Septuagint, Syriac, and Arabic add, “of her husband. But she that hateth righteousness is a throne of disgrace. The slothful, though rich, shall come to poverty; but the laborious shall retain their riches.” Two hemistics seem to be lost in Hebrew. (Kennicott) — Most of the additions in the Septuagint are only glosses, or useless repetitions, (Calmet) though they seem not to be so in this place. (Haydock)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Kindred. Hebrew, “flesh,” Genesis xxix. 14. The miser is cruel even to himself."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","In hand. At rest, or making agreements. God will punish the race of the wicked."],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Foolish. Beauty, without prudence, leads to ruin, as ornaments are ill bestowed on swine. The women in the east sometimes wore rings in their noses, (Genesis xxiv. 22.; Calmet) or hanging down upon them, Isaias iii. 21. (Menochius)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Others. Moderation is always requisite. Hebrew, “there is one withholding from rectitude, yet for a defect,” (Mont.[Montanus?]) being too saving, he is a loser. (Haydock) — Avarice does not always increase riches. (Calmet)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Himself. He shall receive abundantly, 2 Corinthians ix. 6. The beneficent shall be amply rewarded both in this world and in the next."],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Corn. In times of scarcity. See Amos viii. 7."],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","House. By his profligacy, or law-suits, shall be impoverished, (ver. 17.; Calmet) or if he act with violence, he will make his house empty, Ecclesiasticus iv. 35. (Menochius)"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Life. Producing excellent fruits of virtue and edification."],["haydock",31,null,"haydock","Receive. Punishment, for almost inevitable faults, or be treated according to his deserts. Septuagint, Syriac, and Arabic, “if the just be hardly saved, where shall the impious and the sinner appear?” (1 Peter iv. 18.) (Calmet) — Afflictions attend the just in this life. Shall the wicked escape? (Menochius)"]],"au":{"bede":["Bede the Venerable",1,1,735,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#bede"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[15,null,0,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part I","1. First Conference of Abbot Moses",0,435,"… Of which kind of illusions it is well said in the Proverbs: \"There are ways which seem to be right to a man, but their latter end will come into the depths of hell,\" and again \"An evil man is harmful when he attaches himself to a good man,\" i.e., the devil deceives when he is covered with an appearance of sanctity: \"but he hates the sound of the watchman,\" i.e., the power of discretion which comes from the words and warnings of the fathers."],[22,null,1,"On Virginity","On Virginity, Chapter XVII",1,395,"… Let the virtuous life have for its substructure the love of virginity; but upon this let every result of virtue be reared. If virginity is believed to be a vastly precious thing and to have a divine look (as indeed is the case, as well as men believe of it), yet, if the whole life does not harmonize with this perfect note, and it be marred by the succeeding discord of the soul, this thing becomes but \"the jewel of gold in the swine's snout\" or \"the pearl that is trodden under the swine's feet.\""],[22,null,0,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part II","14. The First Conference of Abbot Nesteros",0,435,"… Of those then who seem to acquire some show of knowledge or of those who while they devote themselves diligently to reading the sacred volume and to committing the Scriptures to memory, yet forsake not carnal sins, it is well said in Proverbs: \"Like as a golden ring in a swine's snout so is the beauty of an evil-disposed woman. For what does it profit a man to gain the ornaments of heavenly eloquence and the most precious beauty of the Scriptures if by clinging to filthy deeds and thoughts he destroys it by burying it in the foulest ground, or defiles it by the dirty wallowing of his own lusts? …"],[26,null,2,"The Book of Pastoral Rule","Part 3",2,604,"It is well said through Solomon, \"He that hideth corn shall be cursed among the people.\" For to hide corn is to retain with one's self the words of sacred preaching. And every one that does so is cursed among the people, because through his fault of silence only he is condemned in the punishment of the many whom he might have corrected."],[30,null,3,"Fragments - Exegetical. Fragments from Commentaries on Various Books of Scripture","Hippolytus Exegetical Fragments",3,235,"The fruit of righteousness and the tree of life is Christ. He alone, as man, fulfilled all righteousness. And with His own underived life He has brought forth the fruits of knowledge and virtue like a tree, whereof they that eat shall receive eternal life, and shall enjoy the tree of life in paradise, with Adam and all the righteous. But the souls of the unrighteous meet an untimely expulsion from the presence of God, by whom they shall be left to remain in the flame of torment."]],"fau":[["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["gregory-nyssa","Gregory of Nyssa",0,"father",null,1,395],["gregory-great","Gregory the Great",1,"father",null,1,604],["hippolytus-of-rome","Hippolytus of Rome",0,"father",null,0]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 5 (1893)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf205"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 12 (1895)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf212"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 5 (1886)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf05"]]}